(Stage 1d — a recursive function taking `a value` must keep that value's
 runtime tag correct across many call frames. `walk` recurses 100 deep,
 threading `v` (a value) through every call; at the base it asks `v is a
 number`. If any frame dropped or corrupted the tag, a text/decimal `v`
 would mis-classify as a number. Acceptance 4.)

To "walk" with a value called "v" and a number called "n".
  If n is greater than 0, return a number, "walk" of v and n subtract 1.
  Otherwise if v is a number, return a number, 1.
  Otherwise return a number, 0.

print "walk" of 1 and 100.
print "walk" of "two" and 100.
print "walk" of 3.5 and 100.